**What this means:** The emerald premium in natural stones is real and grows with size. At 1ct, it is meaningful but manageable ($676–$1,450 more). At 3ct, the price gap becomes enormous — a 3ct emerald F-VS1 costs $26,583 more than a 3ct-range asscher at F-VVS2.

Buyers who prefer emerald at 3ct+ need to significantly increase their budget over equivalent asscher specs.

**Verdict:** For natural buyers on a budget, asscher delivers equal step-cut aesthetics and identical optical behavior at a substantially lower price at every size. The emerald premium is not about quality — it is about shape demand. If you must have the rectangular corridor look, budget for it.

If you love step cuts but are flexible on shape, asscher is the better value.

> **Farzana says:** The price gap at 3ct always surprises buyers. They come in thinking emerald and asscher are the same price because they're both step cuts. At 1ct the difference is a few hundred dollars. At 3ct it's twenty-six thousand. Emerald is just more popular — more demand, higher price. If the rectangular corridors speak to you, buy emerald and budget for it. If you can live with the square, buy asscher and spend the difference on clarity or size.

